<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) hold promise for integrating diverse data modalities, but current medical adaptations—such as LLaVA-Med—often fail to fully exploit the synergy between color fundus photography (CFP) and optical coherence tomography (OCT), and offer limited interpretability of quantitative biomarkers. We introduce <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GROK</span>, the first MLLM that jointly processes CFP, OCT, and text to deliver clinician-grade diagnoses of ocular and systemic disease. GROK comprises three core modules—Knowledge-Guided Instruction Generation, CLIP-Style OCT–Biomarker Alignment, and Supervised Instruction Fine-Tuning—that together instill a quantitative-to-qualitative diagnostic chain-of-thought, mirroring real clinical reasoning when producing detailed lesion annotations.
To evaluate our approach, we introduce the Grounded Ophthalmic Understanding benchmark, which covers six disease categories and three tasks—macro-level diagnostic classification, report generation quality, and fine-grained clinical quality assessment of the generated chain-of-thought. Experiments show that, with only LoRA (Low-Rank Adaptation) fine-tuning of a 7B-parameter Qwen2 backbone, GROK outperforms comparable 7B and 32B baselines on both report quality and fine-grained clinical metrics—and even exceeds OpenAI-o3. Our code and data are released at <a href="https://anonymous.4open.science/r/GROK-A-Retinal-MMLM-3F30" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href ltx_font_typewriter">GROK’s repository</a>.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Index Terms: </h6>Multimodal Large Language Models, Color fundus photography, Optical coherence tomograph.

</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Introduction</span></h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Ocular biomarkers have been extensively validated as indicators of
ophthalmic diseases—such as diabetic retinopathy and glaucoma
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>—and of systemic
diseases, including hypertension and neurodegeneration
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. Color fundus photography (CFP) provides pigment-rich, en-face views,
whereas optical coherence tomography (OCT) offers high-resolution
cross-sections; together they capture complementary retinal pathology <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.04281v1/figure_1.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/279;" width="476" height="279" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> Motivating example. On paired CFP &amp; OCT images, existing MLLMs (Lingshu-32B, Qwen2.5VL-32B, OpenAI-o3; left) each fail one of three criteria—quantitative biomarker analysis, qualitative diagnosis (sub-inference), or alignment with the final conclusion (✗). Our pipeline (right) meets all three (✓), converting measurements into coherent clinical reasoning.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Traditional clinical pipelines combine automated segmentation of retinal structures (vessels, optic disc, layers) with clinician review. The leading CFP tool, AutoMorph <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, quantifies biomarkers like vessel calibre and tortuosity. However, most existing methods remain unimodal (OCT or CFP).
For example, in diabetic retinopathy, CFP detects micro-aneurysms and hemorrhages, whereas OCT measures retinal nerve fiber layer (RNFL) thickness and macular volume, which limits their combined diagnostic accuracy. Moreover, downstream validation still relies heavily on expert analysis, impeding large-scale deployment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Recent retinal foundation models such as RETFound <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> and RetiZero <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> have markedly boosted diagnostic accuracy, yet they function essentially as black-box classifiers, providing little easy-to-understand interpretability. The emergence of medical MLLMs has partially mitigated this gap. Leveraging their vast medical pre-trained corpora and powerful multi-modal capabilities, these models can articulate the entire diagnostic process in natural language. Systems such as <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LLaVA-Med</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>,
<span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Lingshu</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>,
<span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">OphGLM</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, and
<span id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">RetinaVLM</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> have pushed ophthalmic
and systemic disease detection toward expert-level visual reasoning. Nevertheless, a critical limitation remains: <span id="S1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Limited clinician-friendly interpretability.</span> A clinician–friendly and trustworthy ophthalmic MLLM should therefore jointly model CFP and OCT images. It must be able to <em id="S1.p3.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">quantify abnormal biomarkers</em> present in each modality, <em id="S1.p3.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">derive qualitative sub-inferences</em> from those abnormal measurements, and finally <em id="S1.p3.1.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">align the resulting sub-inferences with the overall clinical conclusion</em>. As <a href="#S1.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ I Introduction ‣ GROK: From Quantitative Biomarkers to Qualitative Diagnosis via a Grounded MLLM with Knowledge-Guided Instruction" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 1</span></a> illustrates, existing medical MLLMs can generate
textual explanations for retinal images, yet they still suffer from three
key limitations:
(i) they seldom provide <em id="S1.p3.1.9" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">quantitative analyses</em> of abnormal biomarkers;
(ii) they struggle to convert those numbers into <em id="S1.p3.1.10" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">qualitative
sub-inferences</em> with clear clinical meaning; and
(iii) the produced sub-inferences are often inconsistent with the final
diagnostic conclusion, breaking the reasoning chain. These shortcomings hinder the safe, large-scale clinical deployment of current ophthalmic MLLMs.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.04281v1/figure_2.png" id="S1.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/278;" width="476" height="278" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 2</span>: </span><span id="S1.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Illustration of GROK’s model architecture. It consists of two clip-style vision encoders (for CFP and OCT), followed by projection layers that align the visual features with the text embedding space before being processed by the LLM. The model’s effectiveness relies on a three-stage training pipeline: Knowledge-Guided Instruction Generation, OCT–Biomarker Alignment, and Supervised Instruction Fine-tuning.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To address these critical limitations, we introduce <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GROK</span>—
a MLLM for grounded retinal image understanding that jointly interprets CFP and OCT images, transforms quantitative biomarkers into
qualitative sub-inferences, and unifies them into a coherent
diagnostic report. As the first AI assistant for disease diagnosis based on fundus data to learn via knowledge-guided instructions, GROK differs from existing medical MLLMs in three key ways. First, we extracted 31 OCT and 6 CFP biomarkers from UKBiobank samples.
Next, these quantitative features were fed into an expert-crafted
“Eye-Guideline” prompt template and processed by the OpenAI-o3
language model, producing a <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">biomarker-based analysis</span> that links every
measurement to its corresponding diagnostic conclusion. Second, GROK provides an efficient framework: it first extracts abnormal ocular biomarkers and derives sub-inferences from them, then integrates these sub-inferences into the final prediction, thereby achieving <span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">quantitative-to-qualitative chain-of-thought reasoning</span>. Third, <span id="S1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Clinician-level transparency.</span> By mirroring step-by-step
diagnostic reasoning—linking each biomarker to its sub-inference and
logically aggregating these into the final diagnosis—GROK produces
an end-to-end, biomarker-anchored rationale that is fully transparent
and clinically verifiable.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To realize these capabilities, GROK’s modular architecture comprises three key components: <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Knowledge-Guided Instruction.</span> As shown in <a href="#S3.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ III-B Knowledge Guided Instruction Generation ‣ III METHODS ‣ GROK: From Quantitative Biomarkers to Qualitative Diagnosis via a Grounded MLLM with Knowledge-Guided Instruction" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 3</span></a> (1), we construct the Ophthalmic Interpretation Benchmark,
which contains 15 663 UKBiobank CFP–OCT pairs, each annotated with 31 OCT and 6 CFP biomarkers.
These biomarkers are inserted into an doctor-verified <em id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Eye-Guideline</em> prompt template;
the completed prompts are then fed to OpenAI-o3, which returns a fine-grained
chain-of-thought that traces how each quantitative abnormality supports the final diagnosis.
The benchmark thus supplies supervision for GROK to learn quantitative-to-qualitative
reasoning. <span id="S1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Semantic Alignment.</span>
Off-the-shelf CLIP-style encoders do not exist for volumetric OCT data.
We therefore pre-train an OCT backbone by contrastively aligning
the embeddings of 2-D central-foveal OCT B-scan images with their
corresponding 3-D biomarker vectors, grounding each slice in
clinically meaningful features and supplying rich representations
for downstream multimodal reasoning. <span id="S1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Cross-Modal Fusion.</span> A shared projection layer maps CFP and OCT embeddings into a unified, language-compatible space; concatenated with prompt tokens and fed into the LLM’s next-token head, this enables the model to seamlessly fuse and explain OCT and CFP findings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The main contributions of this work are:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Grounded Retinal MLLMs.</span> We present GROK, the first knowledge-guided multimodal retinal MLLM. GROK couples dual CLIP-style encoders for CFP and OCT with a cross-modal fusion projector that maps both visual streams into the language-model embedding space, and it is instruction-tuned on our fine-granularity Ophthalmic Interpretation Benchmark —enabling the LLMs to emulate clinician reasoning and to perform quantitative-to-qualitative chain-of-thought inference.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Ophthalmic Interpretation Benchmark.</span> We release a fully reproducible pipeline—including biomarker-extraction
code and the Eye-Guideline prompt templates that can be used to
generate the 37 biomarker-based instruction–response pairs from
UKBiobank CFP/OCT scans. Building on these resources, we establish an
evaluation suite that assesses grounded diagnosis, feature grounding, and
decision reliability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Multimodal Alignment and Cross-Modal Fusion Mechanisms.</span> To compensate for the lack of CLIP-style encoders for volumetric OCT,
we contrastively align 2-D OCT-slice embeddings with their 3-D biomarker
vectors and project both OCT and CFP features into a shared space,
yielding clinically grounded representations that power downstream
LLMs training and multimodal diagnosis.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Related Work</span></h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">II-A</span> </span><span id="S2.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Multimodal Large Language Models</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we formulate the task as conditioned report generation
given a CFP–OCT pair and a standardized diagnostic instruction, which can
be regarded as a medical visual-question-answering (VQA) instance: the
instruction serves as the question, and the generated report as the
answer.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Early VQA research centres on natural photography, where networks such
as BAN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> and MCAN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> employ
bilinear and modular co-attention mechanisms that explicitly match
question words to salient image regions. Cross-modal Transformers soon
followed: ViLBERT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> and
UNITER <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> were pre-trained on large-scale
image–text pairs, support open-ended queries, and became strong
baselines for downstream VQA and retrieval tasks. In the medical
domain, MedViLL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> and
BioViL-T <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> adapt this paradigm to chest
X-ray or CT, yet they remain <em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">single-modality</em> and return only brief
textual answers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">More recent systems such as BLIP-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> and
LLaVA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> inject visual features directly into a
frozen LLM backbone, enabling free-form image–text dialogue far beyond
the short answers produced by classic VQA networks. In healthcare,
existing MLLMs typically focus on either imaging (e.g. radiology
X-rays, CT scans <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>) or physiological signals (e.g. ECG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>) in isolation. Yet no study attempts to ground
CFP–OCT representations in biomarker measurements and to generate an
interpretable, chain-of-thought diagnosis—a gap that our GROK framework
fills.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span> </span><span id="S2.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">MLLMs-based Ophthalmic Data Analysis</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">OCT and CFP are fundamental ophthalmic modalities, and integrating these
images with clinical text via MLLMs has recently gained traction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">OphGLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> fine-tunes a lightweight fusion module on
the expert-annotated FundusTuning-CN dataset, outperforming general
vision–language baselines in fundus-disease classification and clinical
dialogue. RetinaVLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> aligns
self-supervised OCT features with LLaMA-3 by curriculum training and a
shared projection head, achieving junior-ophthalmologist–level
biomarker identification and staging. Lingshu <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>
extends Qwen2.5-VL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> through
multi-stage training and reinforcement learning on a large-scale
medical multimodal corpus, thereby improving performance on general
clinical tasks. However, these models remain largely black-box
input-to-output systems and do not provide an explicit
quantitative-to-qualitative reasoning chain, rendering their diagnostic
processes opaque.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Most existing ophthalmic MLLMs either process a single imaging modality
or output a diagnosis without a transparent sub-inference chain, which
limits interpretability and clinical trust. <span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GROK</span> addresses
this gap by jointly modelling CFP and OCT with explicit,
biomarker-anchored reasoning, yielding an interpretable
quantitative-to-qualitative chain of thought and thereby enhancing
trustworthiness.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">METHODS</span></h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Overview:</span> Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ I Introduction ‣ GROK: From Quantitative Biomarkers to Qualitative Diagnosis via a Grounded MLLM with Knowledge-Guided Instruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> depicts the GROK architecture and training
workflow. The design follows the LLaVA paradigm but extends it with
<em id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">two</em> CLIP-style encoders—one for CFP, one for OCT. Features from each modality are first encoded and projected into the
LLM’s token space; the resulting visual tokens are concatenated with the
textual query and then decoded by the language model (Qwen2) to produce a
fine-grained diagnostic report.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This capability is enabled by a carefully designed three-stage training pipeline: <span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage I: Knowledge-guided instruction generation.</span>
We design a domain-specific prompting strategy, named <span id="S3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Eye-guideline</span>, to guide the powerful OpenAI-o3 model in generating structured diagnostic responses based on patients’ data, which are subsequently used as instruction (training) data. <span id="S3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage II: CLIP-style OCT–biomarker alignment.</span>
We train a CLIP-style visual encoder to align OCT with their corresponding fine-grained biomarkers recorded in the UKBiobank, thereby grounding OCT representations in quantitative clinical attributes. <span id="S3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage III: Supervised instruction fine-tuning.</span> In this stage, the LLM is jointly fine-tuned with the Cross-Modal Fusion modules using the instruction data generated in Stage I. The Cross-Modal Fusion modules are responsible for mapping CFP and OCT features into the text embedding space.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">III-A</span> </span><span id="S3.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Preliminaries</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{D}=\{I^{o}_{n},I^{c}_{n},B_{n}\}^{N}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐃</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>o</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{D}=\{I^{o}_{n},I^{c}_{n},B_{n}\}^{N}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> denote a multimodal dataset that contains <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> pairs of CFP <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{c}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{c}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, and OCT <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{o}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>o</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{o}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> are corresponding clinical biomarkers of both. We define the <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{o}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>o</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{o}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> as the OCT embedding of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{o}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>o</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{o}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> from the OCT encoder <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{o}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{o}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{t}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{t}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> as the biomarker embedding from a MLP (multilayer perceptron) -based tabular encoder <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{t}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{t}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>.
We also define the CFP encoder as <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{c}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{c}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{c}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{c}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the embedding of CFP encoder.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Knowledge Guided Instruction
Generation</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We carefully designed the instructions (training data) generation process for training the GROK model, as shown in <a href="#S3.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ III-B Knowledge Guided Instruction Generation ‣ III METHODS ‣ GROK: From Quantitative Biomarkers to Qualitative Diagnosis via a Grounded MLLM with Knowledge-Guided Instruction" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 3</span></a> (1). The knowledge-guided aspect of our method lies chiefly in the instruction-generation stage: we design a doctor-verified Eye-Guideline prompt that embeds clinical expertise and steers OpenAI-o3 to produce a fine-grained chain of thought that links every quantitative abnormality to the final diagnosis.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This template enables OpenAI-o3 to leverage its vast built-in medical knowledge and robust chain-of-thought reasoning capability, fostering reliable, evidence-based links between quantitative information and qualitative interpretations (sub-inference) while explicitly aligning each sub-inference with the final diagnosis—thereby reducing hallucinations and strengthening clinical plausibility.
Another key aspect of this process lies in the query components. Beyond ophthalmic images, we incorporate clinically interpretable OCT and CFP biomarkers, which drive a <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“Quantitative Biomarkers to Qualitative Diagnosis”</span> reasoning pipeline. These biomarkers are fused with visual information to construct intermediate inferences that iteratively lead to final diagnostic conclusions, embodying a structured chain-of-thought based on clinical evidence.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.04281v1/figure_3.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/232;" width="476" height="232" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 3</span>: </span><span id="S3.F3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Knowledge-Guided Instruction Generation:<span id="S3.F3.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Eye-Guideline prompts and OpenAI-o3 convert CFP/OCT biomarkers into a grounded chain-of-thought report.
</span>CLIP-Style OCT-biomarker alignment:<span id="S3.F3.7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> A contrastive loss aligns 2-D central-foveal OCT slices with their 3-D biomarker vectors, yielding clinically grounded OCT features.
</span>Cross-Modal Fusion:<span id="S3.F3.7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Modality-specific projectors embed CFP and OCT features into a shared language space, enabling Qwen2 to generate the final diagnosis.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The OCT biomarkers are derived from validated structural metrics in UKBiobank Category ID 100079, including macular thickness, volume, area, and nerve layer thickness across multiple ETDRS subfields. After filtering out biomarkers with more than 20% missing values, 31 high-quality OCT biomarkers were retained. In addition, 6 CFP biomarkers, including vertical cup-to-disc ratio, arteriovenous ratio, fractal dimension of arteries and veins, and vessel tortuosity, were extracted using a custom pipeline (this will be introduced in Section 4).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To further prevent misdiagnosis and the generation of irrelevant reasoning chains, we also provided each patient’s diagnostic description <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> during the query phase. These labels serve as soft constraints to guide the instruction output generation toward clinically grounded reasoning. Detailed data processing procedures are described in Section <a href="#S4" title="IV EXPERIMENTS AND RESULTS ‣ GROK: From Quantitative Biomarkers to Qualitative Diagnosis via a Grounded MLLM with Knowledge-Guided Instruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a>. Here we define our instruction dataset as: <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{y,I^{o},I^{c},B,g\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>I</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>I</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{y,I^{o},I^{c},B,g\}</annotation></semantics></math>. This whole procedure can be formulated as:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{n}=\mathrm{OpenAI\text{-}o3}\bigl(\mathrm{EyeGuideline}(I^{o}_{n},\,I^{c}_{n},B_{n},g_{n})\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>OpenAI</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o3</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>EyeGuideline</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>o</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{n}=\mathrm{OpenAI\text{-}o3}\bigl(\mathrm{EyeGuideline}(I^{o}_{n},\,I^{c}_{n},B_{n},g_{n})\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">III-C</span> </span><span id="S3.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CLIP‑Style OCT–Biomarker Alignment
</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although CLIP-style encoders are well established for CFP—here we adopt
RetiZero <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> as the CFP eencoder—off-the-shelf
CLIP-style encoders do not yet exist for volumetric OCT data.Moreover, our instruction data contain rich quantitative biomarkers;
vanilla CLIP encoders, trained only on qualitative image–text pairs,
are unlikely to infer such numeric measurements. To alleviate this, we use the provided 2-D
central-foveal OCT B-scan images to contrastively align iht heir corresponding
3-D biomarkers. The resulting unified encoder fuses OCT image and biomarkers’ numeric embeddings, improving representation quality and downstream generalization for MLLMs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in <a href="#S3.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ III-B Knowledge Guided Instruction Generation ‣ III METHODS ‣ GROK: From Quantitative Biomarkers to Qualitative Diagnosis via a Grounded MLLM with Knowledge-Guided Instruction" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 3</span></a> (2), we apply a CLIP-style contrastive loss in the shared embedding space to align projections across different modalities, rather than within the same modality. Specifically, we maximize the cosine similarity between matched OCT and biomarker pairs while minimizing the cosine similarity of all the other pairs. Thus the image to biomarkers loss can be express as:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\ell_{i\to t}=-\frac{1}{N}\sum_{j=1}^{N}\log\frac{\exp\!\bigl(\cos(z^{o}_{j},z_{j}^{t})/\tau\bigr)}{\sum_{k=1,k\neq j}^{N}\exp\!\bigl(\cos(z^{o}_{j},z_{k}^{t})/\tau\bigr)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup><mi>log</mi><mfrac><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>o</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>o</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{i\to t}=-\frac{1}{N}\sum_{j=1}^{N}\log\frac{\exp\!\bigl(\cos(z^{o}_{j},z_{j}^{t})/\tau\bigr)}{\sum_{k=1,k\neq j}^{N}\exp\!\bigl(\cos(z^{o}_{j},z_{k}^{t})/\tau\bigr)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is a temperature hyperparameter that scales the cosine similarity scores. <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{i\to t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{i\to t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{t\to i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{t\to i}</annotation></semantics></math> represent image-to-text and text-to-image loss, respectively.
<math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{t\to i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{t\to i}</annotation></semantics></math> is calculated analogously, and the total loss is</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=\frac{1}{2}\bigl(\ell_{i\to t}+\ell_{t\to i}\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=\frac{1}{2}\bigl(\ell_{i\to t}+\ell_{t\to i}\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">III-D</span> </span><span id="S3.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Supervised Instruction Finetuning</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Independent training of OCT and CFP encoders produces embeddings in disparate latent subspaces that are incompatible with the LLM’s text embedding space. To ensure the LLM can interpret OCT and CFP inputs as effectively as text, we project both modalities into the same dimensionality as the textual embedding space, aligning them within a unified semantic representation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As illustrated in  <a href="#S3.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ III-B Knowledge Guided Instruction Generation ‣ III METHODS ‣ GROK: From Quantitative Biomarkers to Qualitative Diagnosis via a Grounded MLLM with Knowledge-Guided Instruction" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 3</span></a> (3), we freeze the pre‑trained encoders <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{o}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{o}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{c}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{c}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding their respective output embeddings <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{\mathrm{o}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{\mathrm{o}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{\mathrm{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{\mathrm{c}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We then project the OCT embeddings into the same dimensionality as the CFP embeddings using an MLP projector with parameter <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{M_{o}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{M_{o}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{z^{\mathrm{o}}}=\mathrm{MLP}_{\mathrm{o}}\bigl(z^{\mathrm{o}};\theta_{M_{\mathrm{o}}}\bigr)\;\in\;\mathbb{R}^{Bs\times d_{m}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>MLP</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.280em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{z^{\mathrm{o}}}=\mathrm{MLP}_{\mathrm{o}}\bigl(z^{\mathrm{o}};\theta_{M_{\mathrm{o}}}\bigr)\;\in\;\mathbb{R}^{Bs\times d_{m}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Subsequently, we employ an additional projector called Feature-to-Text Mapper with parameter <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{M}</annotation></semantics></math> to map both OCT and CFP image representations into a consistent textual space:</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\mathrm{o}}=\mathrm{MLP}_{\mathrm{text}}\bigl(\hat{z^{\mathrm{o}}};\,\theta_{M}\bigr)\;\in\;\mathbb{R}^{Bs\times d_{t}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>MLP</mi><mi>text</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.280em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\mathrm{o}}=\mathrm{MLP}_{\mathrm{text}}\bigl(\hat{z^{\mathrm{o}}};\,\theta_{M}\bigr)\;\in\;\mathbb{R}^{Bs\times d_{t}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\mathrm{c}}=\mathrm{MLP}_{\mathrm{text}}\bigl(z^{\mathrm{c}};\,\theta_{M}\bigr)\;\in\;\mathbb{R}^{Bs\times d_{t}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>MLP</mi><mi>text</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.280em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\mathrm{c}}=\mathrm{MLP}_{\mathrm{text}}\bigl(z^{\mathrm{c}};\,\theta_{M}\bigr)\;\in\;\mathbb{R}^{Bs\times d_{t}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the dimensionality of the text embedding space. The projector transforms both <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{z}^{\mathrm{o}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>o</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{z}^{\mathrm{o}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{\mathrm{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{\mathrm{c}}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{Bs\times d_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{Bs\times d_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math>, aligning them with the LLM’s text embedding space and enabling their seamless interpretation as text‑equivalent inputs.
Subsequently, these projected embeddings <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\mathrm{c}},h^{\mathrm{o}}\in\mathbb{R}^{Bs\times d_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\mathrm{c}},h^{\mathrm{o}}\in\mathbb{R}^{Bs\times d_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> are concatenated with the textual query <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a simply prompt to require the model to interpret the retinal images, to form the joint multimodal representation:</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=\mathrm{Concat}\bigl(h^{\mathrm{c}},\,h^{\mathrm{o}},\,\mathrm{Embed}(x_{q})\bigr)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Concat</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>Embed</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=\mathrm{Concat}\bigl(h^{\mathrm{c}},\,h^{\mathrm{o}},\,\mathrm{Embed}(x_{q})\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">During the supervised finetuning using the knowledge guided generated instructions data, both CFP and OCT encoders are frozen while the projectors and Large Language Model are jointly optimized. The CFP encoder we used is Retizero <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. And the OCT encoder is our biomarker-aligned RETFound backbone <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. For the language model, we utilize Qwen2-7B-Instruct, since this is the latest LLM support provided by the LLaVA framework that we adopted to build MLLMs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\mathrm{LLM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>LLM</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\mathrm{LLM}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the parameters of LLM, the model is trained with a standard auto-regressive objective, which minimizes the negative log-likelihood of the target sequence <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> given the multimodal inputs <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. Formally,</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}\bigl(y;\,\theta_{\mathrm{LLM}}(x)\bigr)=-\sum_{i=1}^{N}\log P\bigl(y_{i}\mid x;\,\theta_{\mathrm{LLM}}\bigr)\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">;</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>LLM</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mi>log</mi><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">;</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>LLM</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}\bigl(y;\,\theta_{\mathrm{LLM}}(x)\bigr)=-\sum_{i=1}^{N}\log P\bigl(y_{i}\mid x;\,\theta_{\mathrm{LLM}}\bigr)\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> represents the length of the target sequence <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> its <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th token.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">EXPERIMENTS AND RESULTS</span></h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Datasets</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We construct a multimodal dataset <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{I^{o}_{n},I^{c}_{n},B_{n},g_{n},y_{n}\}^{N}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>o</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{I^{o}_{n},I^{c}_{n},B_{n},g_{n},y_{n}\}^{N}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> based on the UKBiobank. For each participant (using the unique identifier <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">eid</span>), we extract the left-eye color fundus photograph (Field 21015), <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{c}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{c}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the foveal OCT B-scan (Field 21017), <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{o}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{o}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since UKBiobank does not provide native CFP-based biomarkers, we adopt an EyeQ-inspired preprocessing pipeline <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>: CFP images are quality-filtered using a ResNet model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> trained on EyePACS. High-quality images are then processed by nnUNetv2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> for artery–vein segmentation, and by BI-GCN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> for cup–disc segmentation. Together with OCT’s, each data sample contains 37 biomarkers, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To generate diagnostic labels <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, we aggregate multiple sources within the UKBiobank, including self-reported non-cancer illness codes (Field 20002), hospital episode records with ICD-10 diagnoses, and additional clinical fields. We select six representative ophthalmic and systemic disease categories for analysis (see  <a href="#S4.T1" title="TABLE I ‣ IV-A Datasets ‣ IV EXPERIMENTS AND RESULTS ‣ GROK: From Quantitative Biomarkers to Qualitative Diagnosis via a Grounded MLLM with Knowledge-Guided Instruction" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table I</span></a>). The dataset is split by <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">eid</span> using an 80:20 ratio, resulting in 12,489 training samples and 3,122 testing samples.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">

<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:129%;">TABLE I</span>: </span><span id="S4.T1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:129%;">Distribution (%) of disease categories in the dataset.</span></figcaption><div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<table id="S4.T1.7" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S4.T1.7.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.7.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:3.5pt;padding-right:3.5pt;"><span id="S4.T1.7.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Normal</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.7.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:3.5pt;padding-right:3.5pt;"><span id="S4.T1.7.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Hypertension</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.7.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:3.5pt;padding-right:3.5pt;"><span id="S4.T1.7.1.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Diabetes</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.7.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:3.5pt;padding-right:3.5pt;"><span id="S4.T1.7.1.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Glaucoma</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.7.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:3.5pt;padding-right:3.5pt;"><span id="S4.T1.7.1.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">DR</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.7.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:3.5pt;padding-right:3.5pt;"><span id="S4.T1.7.1.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">AMD</span></td>
