<span id="S4.T2.5.13.5.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">48.20</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.5.13.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">
<span id="S4.T2.5.13.6.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S4.T2.5.13.6.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">47.65</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.5.13.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">
<span id="S4.T2.5.13.7.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S4.T2.5.13.7.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">52.01</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.5.13.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">
<span id="S4.T2.5.13.8.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S4.T2.5.13.8.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">0.87</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.5.13.9" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">
<span id="S4.T2.5.13.9.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S4.T2.5.13.9.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">0.14</span>
</span></td></tr>
</table></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<ul id="S4.I2" class="ltx_itemize ltx_figure_panel">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">GROK (Ours)</span><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">: our method (OCT-biomarker aligned encoder + CFP encoder + Qwen2).
</span><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">GROK-CLIP-ViT</span><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">: replace our encoder with vanilla CLIP-ViT (joint OCT+CFP training).
</span><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">GROK-RETFound-OCT</span><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">: replace OCT-biomarker encoder with RETFound (pretrained with OCT).
</span><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">GROK-Vicuna-7B</span><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">: replace Qwen2 LLM with Vicuna-7B-v1.5.
</span><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">GROK-OCT-only</span><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">: remove CFP branch, keep only OCT encoder + Qwen2.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul></div></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">IV-C</span> </span><span id="S4.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Evaluation</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate GROK’s performance comprehensively, we frame the task as generating an evidence-based diagnostic report from an OCT–CFP pair ( <a href="#S4.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ IV-C Evaluation ‣ IV EXPERIMENTS AND RESULTS ‣ GROK: From Quantitative Biomarkers to Qualitative Diagnosis via a Grounded MLLM with Knowledge-Guided Instruction" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 4</span></a>). Our multi-faceted evaluation covers three aspects:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S4.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Macro-level Diagnostic Classification:</span> At a high level, this task can be framed as a multi-class classification problem and evaluated using Macro F1-Score, which accounts for class imbalance.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Report Generation Quality:</span> To evaluate the fidelity of the generated reports, We employ the BERT-Score, a standard metric that that quantifies the semantic overlap between model outputs and reference reports, ensuring the analytical narrative aligns with clinical grounded report.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I3.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fine-Grained Clinical Quality Assessment:</span> To perform a deeper fine-grained assessment of the clinical utility of the generated reports, we design a <span id="S4.I3.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rubric-based Scoring Framework</span>, implemented with OpenAI-o3 as LLM-judge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> to score reports against templates, ground truth, and biomarkers. It comprises six metrics: <span id="S4.I3.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Quantitative Accuracy</span>, which checks if numeric statements are correct; <span id="S4.I3.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Qualitative Accuracy</span>, which evaluates if descriptive findings are accurate; <span id="S4.I3.i3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Evidence Grounding</span>, which verifies if claims are grounded in evidence; <span id="S4.I3.i3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Reasoning Consistency</span>, which ensures the logic is coherent and non-contradictory; <span id="S4.I3.i3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Coverage Completeness</span>, which checks if the interpretation addresses all key anatomical domains; and <span id="S4.I3.i3.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Error Severity Penalty</span>, which penalizes dangerous or management-changing errors.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We benchmarked GROK against representative baseline models, including: (1) closed-source models, OpenAI-o3 and GPT-4o; (2) the 32B-parameter open-source vision-language model, Qwen2.5-VL<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, and its medically tuned version, Lingshu <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>; (3) their corresponding 7B-parameter versions, matching GROK’s scale. To ensure a fair comparison and mitigate potential style biases from our training data, we standardized prompts across all baseline models, explicitly requiring grounded reasoning and quantitative estimates to align their output styles. The detailed evaluation rubric and implementation are available in our open-source repository.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.04281v1/figure_4.png" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/264;" width="476" height="264" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 4</span>: </span><span id="S4.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison of diagnostic reports generated by GROK and OpenAI-o3 on retinal fundus images from a diabetic retinopathy case.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">IV-D</span> </span><span id="S4.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Experimental Results</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The comprehensive performance of all evaluated models is summarized in  <a href="#S4.T2" title="TABLE II ‣ IV-B Experimental Setting ‣ IV EXPERIMENTS AND RESULTS ‣ GROK: From Quantitative Biomarkers to Qualitative Diagnosis via a Grounded MLLM with Knowledge-Guided Instruction" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table II</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">From a macro-level diagnostic classification perspective</span>, all models exhibit low Macro F1-scores (<math id="S4.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq 0.25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mn>0.25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq 0.25</annotation></semantics></math>), far below clinically deployable thresholds, highlighting the inherent difficulty of multi-class diagnosis on real-world, imbalanced datasets, as shown in  <a href="#S4.T1" title="TABLE I ‣ IV-A Datasets ‣ IV EXPERIMENTS AND RESULTS ‣ GROK: From Quantitative Biomarkers to Qualitative Diagnosis via a Grounded MLLM with Knowledge-Guided Instruction" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table I</span></a>. Despite the general difficulty of multi-class diagnosis under long-tailed label distributions, our 7B model GROK achieves a competitive Macro F1-score of 0.23, matching the performance of the much larger, medically-specialized Lingshu-32B (0.23) and approaching the proprietary OpenAI-o3 (0.25). Notably, GROK significantly outperforms other open-source 7B models such as Qwen2.5-VL (0.19) and Lingshu-7B (0.18), while being trained via lightweight <span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LoRA</span> fine-tuning with only minimal parameter updates.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">From a report generation quality perspective</span>, GROK achieves the highest BERT-score (0.88), compared to GPT-4o (0.85) and OpenAI-o3 (0.87). While this suggests stronger semantic alignment with ground-truth references, it is worth noting that the evaluation is conducted on reports generated in a consistent prompting style with GROK’s training data. Therefore, the BERT-score may partially reflect stylistic alignment rather than general semantic superiority.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">From a fine-grained clinical quality assessment perspective</span>, GROK achieves leading performance across several key dimensions among all open-source models. Specifically, it attains the highest scores in <span id="S4.SS4.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Quantitative Accuracy</span> (78.23), <span id="S4.SS4.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Evidence Grounding</span> (68.70), and <span id="S4.SS4.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Coverage Completeness</span> (65.56), indicating a strong capacity to extract and estimate numerical information, align statements with clinical evidence, and provide comprehensive anatomical coverage. Although OpenAI-o3 demonstrates higher <span id="S4.SS4.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Qualitative Accuracy</span> (47.79 vs. 45.98) and <span id="S4.SS4.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Reasoning Consistency</span> (70.97 vs. 66.42), GROK achieves competitive results in these areas while substantially outperforming all open-source baselines, including both 7B and 32B models. These findings suggest that GROK benefits from structured biomarker pretraining and targeted instruction tuning, which enhance both factual grounding and clinical completeness.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">As illustrated in  <a href="#S4.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ IV-C Evaluation ‣ IV EXPERIMENTS AND RESULTS ‣ GROK: From Quantitative Biomarkers to Qualitative Diagnosis via a Grounded MLLM with Knowledge-Guided Instruction" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 4</span></a>, our model implements a fine-grained chain-of-thought combined with quantitative and qualitative analysis: it first extracts and quantifies OCT biomarkers, then derives sub-inferences from each measurement, and finally synthesizes them into a coherent diagnostic conclusion. In contrast, OpenAI-o3 produces more redundant sentences (red boxes in  <a href="#S4.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ IV-C Evaluation ‣ IV EXPERIMENTS AND RESULTS ‣ GROK: From Quantitative Biomarkers to Qualitative Diagnosis via a Grounded MLLM with Knowledge-Guided Instruction" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 4</span></a>, without linking observations either to sub-inferences or to the final diagnosis. This comparison demonstrates that our approach delivers authentic diagnostic reasoning—closely mirroring how clinicians think—and outperforms in interpretability and clinical fidelity on both report-generation and fine-grained metrics.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS5.6" class="ltx_text">IV-E</span> </span><span id="S4.SS5.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Ablation Study</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although certain metrics—such as macro F1 and fine-grained clinical quality scores—remain suboptimal, our ablation experiments demonstrate the effectiveness of GROK’s architectural design and training methodology.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Domain-specific encoders significantly outperform generic counterparts.</span>
As shown in Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="TABLE II ‣ IV-B Experimental Setting ‣ IV EXPERIMENTS AND RESULTS ‣ GROK: From Quantitative Biomarkers to Qualitative Diagnosis via a Grounded MLLM with Knowledge-Guided Instruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a>, replacing our OCT–CFP–biomarker–aligned encoders with vanilla CLIP-ViT (GROK-CLIP-ViT) causes Quantitative Accuracy to fall from 78.23 to 61.52, Qualitative Accuracy from 45.98 to 40.81, and Macro F1 from 0.23 to 0.19. This underscores the importance of domain alignment for capturing fine-grained retinal features.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">OCT encoders enhance quantitative estimation after aligned with biomarkers embedding.</span>
We further replaced our aligned OCT encoder with RETFound (<span id="S4.SS5.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GROK-RETFound-OCT</span>). This substitution leads to a sharp decline in quantitative accuracy (58.79 vs. 78.23), indicating that generic OCT pretraining is insufficient for precise biomarker inference. To isolate this effect, we additionally fine-tuned both encoders on a regression task involving 31 OCT-related biomarkers. As shown in Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="TABLE III ‣ IV-E Ablation Study ‣ IV EXPERIMENTS AND RESULTS ‣ GROK: From Quantitative Biomarkers to Qualitative Diagnosis via a Grounded MLLM with Knowledge-Guided Instruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a>, our model achieves lower MAE (3.6069 vs. 3.8525), lower RMSE (6.1004 vs. 6.3583), and higher <math id="S4.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> (0.7106 vs. 0.6621), further confirming the benefit of contrastive OCT–biomarker alignment.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">TABLE III</span>: </span><span id="S4.T3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison of Regression Performance (Lower MAE/RMSE and Higher <math id="S4.T3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> Indicate Better Performance)</span></figcaption>
<table id="S4.T3.5" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S4.T3.5.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T3.5.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T3.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.5.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T3.5.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MAE</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.5.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T3.5.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RMSE</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.5.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><math id="S4.T3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{R^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝑹</mi><mn>𝟐</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{R^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T3.5.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T3.5.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">RETFound <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite></td>
<td id="S4.T3.5.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">3.8525</td>
<td id="S4.T3.5.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">6.3583</td>
<td id="S4.T3.5.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.6621</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T3.5.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T3.5.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Ours</td>
<td id="S4.T3.5.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T3.5.3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3.6069</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.5.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T3.5.3.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">6.1004</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.5.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T3.5.3.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.7106</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T3.5.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T3.5.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_tt"></td>
<td id="S4.T3.5.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_border_tt"></td>
<td id="S4.T3.5.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_border_tt"></td>
<td id="S4.T3.5.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_border_tt"></td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multimodal complementarity enhances diagnostic reasoning.</span>
Ablating the CFP branch (GROK-OCT-only) causes Quantitative Accuracy to plummet from 78.23 to 41.13, Qualitative Accuracy from 45.98 to 35.17, Evidence Grounding from 68.70 to 36.31, Reasoning Consistency from 66.42 to 48.20, and Error Penalty from 60.59 to 52.01 (Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="TABLE II ‣ IV-B Experimental Setting ‣ IV EXPERIMENTS AND RESULTS ‣ GROK: From Quantitative Biomarkers to Qualitative Diagnosis via a Grounded MLLM with Knowledge-Guided Instruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a>). Although the CFP encoder benefits from large‐scale CLIP‐style pretraining (e.g., RetiZero <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>) and the OCT encoder is aligned only on biomarker data, neither modality alone suffices. Only the joint OCT–CFP encoding yields precise biomarker extraction and a coherent quantitative-to-qualitative chain-of-thought.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Larger and more advanced language models offer clear benefits.</span>
An earlier version of GROK employed Vicuna-v1.5-7B, which has a vocabulary roughly five times smaller than Qwen2-7B. As reported in Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="TABLE II ‣ IV-B Experimental Setting ‣ IV EXPERIMENTS AND RESULTS ‣ GROK: From Quantitative Biomarkers to Qualitative Diagnosis via a Grounded MLLM with Knowledge-Guided Instruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a>, this led to markedly lower performance across metrics. Our use of Qwen2 was constrained by LLaVA’s framework compatibility at the time. Fine-tuning with newer, stronger multimodal LLMs is a promising direction for future work.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">V </span><span id="S5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Limitations Analysis</span></h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This study has four main limitations. First, the absence of publicly available paired CFP–OCT datasets forced us to rely solely on UKBiobank dataset, resulting in pronounced class imbalance and a long-tail disease distribution. Second, our CLIP-style pretraining aligns only central-foveal 2D B-scans with 3D biomarkers, omitting volumetric context and thus limiting spatial feature capture. Third, limited computational resources prevented use of larger backbones or full-model fine-tuning, capping potential performance gains. Fourth, obtaining detailed clinical feedback requires additional coordination; we will conduct extensive clinician evaluations in future work to address these gaps.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VI </span><span id="S6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CONCLUSION</span></h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we introduce GROK, a multimodal retinal MLLM that jointly processes CFP, OCT, and text for clinician-grade diagnosis of ocular and systemic disease. GROK uses a CLIP-style dual-encoder, cross-modal alignment, and LoRA-based knowledge-guided fine-tuning to implement a quantitative-to-qualitative chain-of-thought, greatly enhancing interpretability. Compared to traditional eyes-analysis pipelines, GROK reduces expert analysis; versus other medical MLLMs such as Lingshu, it offers superior readability and clinical explainability. On our Grounded Ophthalmic Understanding benchmark, GROK surpasses 7B- and 32B-parameter baselines in diagnostic classification, report quality, and fine-grained clinical assessment, and outperforms OpenAI-o3 on key metrics. These results confirm that domain-aligned dual encoders plus knowledge-guided instruction substantially improve diagnostic robustness and fidelity. Future work will integrate additional imaging modalities and explore full-model fine-tuning on larger backbones to further boost performance and explainability.</p>
</div>
</section>
